  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 137 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSaturday night-Monday...As the longwave trough that has been \nstationary over central Canada finally propagates eastward, zonal \nupper-level flow over the Northern Rockies and Plains returns, \nshifting the direction of the storm track along with it. A subtle \nshortwave is expected to deepen a low pressure along the leeward \nside of the Northern Rockies and Front Range Saturday into Saturday \nnight. This low pressure system will help generate a warm front \nalong the pressure gradient from the high pressure over the region \nthat will be pulled northward into Iowa and western Illinois. \nThe frontal passage and convergence along a developing LLJ is \nlikely to generate scattered showers with embedded thunderstorms\n(50-70% coverage) Saturday night into Sunday and set the stage \nfor a warming and moistening trend early next week. By Monday, \nthe low pressure moves across northern Iowa and southern \nMinnesota into Wisconsin, keeping storm chances on the table \nand pushing a cold front southward. These storms do have a \npotential to turn severe, especially in northern Iowa, where WAA\nand low-level wind shear close to the low pressure center could\nenable some convective organization, but it is too early to \ndetermine the severity. What is more certain is the front \nstalling out across the CWA Monday night into Tuesday as \ndynamical support is lost.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...Another weak shortwave moves across the Central \nRockies, spawning a Front Range low on the tail-end of the \nstationary front draped across the Central Plains and Midwest. \nThis could lead to a deformation zone to form near the surface \nacross the CWA and allow for rounds of storms to develop but \nalso pool moisture (and therefore, dew points). While it is \ndifficult to address the excessive heat and/or severe potential\nthis far out, there is growing confidence (50-70% chance per \n05.12z LREF guidance) that dew points will once again be back in\nthe low-to-mid 70s making conditions outdoors feel muggy for \nmiddle of next week.",
